3. How to Install and Run
-------------------------
Nothing has to be installed, the program is built straight from source.

    cd <the folder containing this project>
    g++ main.cpp Semaphore.cpp -o lab_safebuffer -std=c++11 -pthread
    ./lab_safebuffer

The program stops by itself after all items have been moved and prints
PASS or FAIL as its result. It takes about one second.


4. Required Libraries and Platform Issues
-----------------------------------------
C++11 and the pthread library (-pthread). Nothing else is used.

Tested on Ubuntu 24.04 (WSL2 on Windows 11) with g++ 15. The sources use
only <thread>, <mutex>, <condition_variable>, <chrono> and the standard
containers, so they also build on Linux, macOS and Windows.

Things that caused problems:
 - Template member functions must be defined in the header. The timed
   Wait() in Semaphore is therefore written inline in Semaphore.h; when
   it sat in Semaphore.cpp the timed overload would not link.
 - Semaphore holds a std::mutex and a std::condition_variable, so it can
   not be copied or moved. That is why SafeBuffer is created once in
   main.cpp and shared by the threads instead of being passed by value.
 - Event and SafeBuffer are written inside their headers so the build
   stays at two .cpp files.


5. TO DO / Known Issues
-----------------------
Done:
 [x] Semaphore usable from more than one file (timed Wait moved to the header)
 [x] Event implemented (Wait, Signal, Reset)
 [x] SafeBuffer implemented (bounded buffer, blocks when full or empty)
 [x] main.cpp rewritten to use SafeBuffer and Event
 [x] program checks its own result and prints PASS or FAIL
 [x] README and licence

Not done:
 - SafeBuffer only stores ints.
 - There is no Remove(timeout), so a consumer blocked on an empty buffer
   can not be cancelled.
 - The number of threads and items is fixed at compile time.
 - The "(buffer x/5)" value is read just after the operation while other
   threads are still running, so it can skip a value. This is expected.


6. Files
--------
LICENSE       - GPL v3 terms.
README        - this file.
Semaphore.h   - Semaphore class, with the timed Wait() inline in the header.
Semaphore.cpp - Semaphore::Wait() and Semaphore::Signal().
Event.h       - Event: Wait() blocks, Signal() sets the flag and wakes every
                waiting thread, Reset() clears it.
SafeBuffer.h  - SafeBuffer: bounded buffer of ints with Add(), Remove(),
                Count() and Capacity().
main.cpp      - three producers and two consumers around one SafeBuffer.


7. Other Information
--------------------
How SafeBuffer works
  Three semaphores are used:
    m_spaces - number of free slots, starts at the buffer size
    m_items  - number of stored items, starts at 0
    m_mutex  - starts at 1, so only one thread is inside at a time
  Add() waits for a free slot, takes m_mutex, writes the item and moves
  the write index, releases m_mutex, then signals that an item is ready.
  Remove() does the opposite: it waits for an item, takes m_mutex, reads
  the item and moves the read index, releases m_mutex, then signals that
  a slot is free.
  The waits on m_spaces and m_items are outside m_mutex on purpose. If a
  thread held m_mutex while waiting, a full buffer would deadlock, because
  the consumers could never get in to empty it.

How Event works
  A bool plus a condition variable. Signal() sets the flag and calls
  notify_all(), and the flag stays set, so every thread that is already
  waiting continues and later callers do not block. Reset() clears the
  flag again. In this program the event is used as a start gate: all
  threads are created first, they all stop at startEvent.Wait(), and then
  main releases them together.

What the run shows
  Producers are faster than consumers, so the buffer reaches 5/5 and the
  producers have to wait for free slots. At the end the consumers take
  the last items out and the buffer goes back to 0/5.
  Every item is numbered with its producer id, so the sum of everything
  produced must equal the sum of everything consumed. The program
  compares the two sums and the final buffer size, then prints PASS.
  A normal run produces and consumes 12 items with a sum of 2418.

Testing further
  Change ITEMS_PER_PRODUCER, ITEMS_PER_CONSUMER or the thread counts at
  the top of main.cpp and run again. The PASS line still checks that the
  two sums are equal and that the buffer ends up empty.
